  {
    "episode_id": 91985,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91985.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 15,
    "turn_text": "You know, I had just graduated from high school, born and raised on a farm. Opportunities didn't seem to be plentiful for me. And plus I was young. I had started to school early. I graduated in June. I didn't turn 18 until November. So I was barely old enough to join the Air Force anyway. And so many of the jobs that were available to my high school graduating class had already been taken up by the time I turned 18. I saw the Air Force as an option. We had a strong military legacy in my family. And my mother had remarried. My stepfather was Air Force, so I said, look, I'll go give this a shot. Never dreamed in my wildest imagination that I would wind up staying 26 and a half years. Was it the influence of your stepfather in particular that led you towards the Air Force? You know, I think when my mother remarried and we left the farm, I found out there was a big world out there. And I became enamored with the airplanes and with the lifestyle and with the camaraderie and the family sense that you felt on the military, on the Air Force installations. Our first place that we lived after my mother remarried was Chinud Air Force Base in Illinois. And I'd never lived in a big city before. I mean, I was born and raised on a two-wheel wagon-run mule farm, no indoor plumbing for much of my childhood. And so that was a big change for me. And so it was very,",
