  {
    "episode_id": 91985,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91985.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 28,
    "turn_text": "It was better than I thought it was going to be. I had the opportunity to instruct and to work alongside some great Americans. And it was a senior master sergeant that took a special interest in me. My mother was, until she married my stepfather, and that relationship didn't work either. But for the most part I was raised by a single mom. And so I didn't have strong father figures in my life. And at that time he was a senior master sergeant, Russ Jarvis, took a very, very special interest in me. And he called me in one day and he said, look, I'm going to be putting you up for airmen of the quarter. I want you to to start showing some self-motivation. Do some things that I can write about. So it shows that you're interested in bettering yourself. So he sent me to the education office and we filled out a lot of paperwork and I started taking a few night classes in the following year to my utter amazement. I was selected for an ROTC scholarship. I did not know that the technical school that I had been to that lasted some nine months had given me almost two years of community college of the Air Force Credit. That combined with the night schools that I took, I had nearly two years of formal college education and so I qualified for an ROTC scholarship and everything changed at that point. What did you study in those nine months? Anything in particular? Well, I was doing just general studies, you know, the maths, the English, the histories, the general studies type things, but the funny thing was when the Air Force elected me for the scholarship, they asked me what I wanted to major in and what job I wanted. And I almost couldn't believe that this was happening to me. So I said, well, I'm major in sociology or history because I didn't feel very confident in mathematics and those kinds of things and the sciences. And I'll be a missile launch officer. Nobody wanted to be a missile launch officer. That was the last thing that newly commissioned lieutenants wanted to do. And so when they gave me the answer to my questions, they said, well, no, you're not going to major in sociology. You're going to major in computer science. And you're going to pilot training. And I said, you've got to be kidding me because I had scored like 95% on the pilot part of the AFOQT. So it was 85 or 95. I forget the exact number. But it was a pretty high score. And I couldn't believe what was about to happen.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
