    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91985.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 35,
    "turn_text": "It was. It was. I didn't graduate from flight school. I had an inner-ear problem that grounded me. And one of the biggest disappointments that I've ever had in my professional career when I was a senior at Troy, which is about 45 miles south of Montgomery, Alabama, it's about 30 miles north of Enterprise, Alabama. Enterprise, Alabama, were the Army and the Air Force do their helicopter pilot training. So they had offered my graduating class of pilot candidates the opportunity to go to helicopter pilot training right away, get commissioned one week and within a couple of weeks your own active duty and starting your pilot training. I wish I had taken that because the the inner ear problem that ultimately grounded me would never have manifested itself in helicopter flying. It's just a different kind of physiological effects on your body. I love to fly and I wish I had taken them up on that offer. Where did you go once flight school didn't work out? Because I had a degree in computer science, they kept me. Computer science turned out to be something that I adapted to very, very quickly and very well. So I became a software engineer.",
