    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91985.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 41,
    "turn_text": "for The Air Force was looking for a lot of software engineers. Computer programming was a really big thing. So I started out as a Fortran programmer, a cobalt programmer at Randolph Air Force Base in Texas. It was there for two years when Another education opportunity cropped up that I was totally unaware was going to come my way. I was selected for what was then called a senior commander sponsored education program. Each four star in the Air Force could select two officers from their command to go to a civilian school to get an advanced degree. And I didn't even know I was being considered. I found out about it quite happenstance, but I got selected and went to Georgia Tech to get a graduate degree in computer science, and then became a program manager and started developing large-scale networks and implementing large-scale projects. It was a fantastic career that certainly translated later after I retired into the businesses that I started. How different was it building a network than compared to now? Much, much different. When I came along in the Air Force, we did a lot of things organically. We had a lot of computer programmers. a lot of the software that ran the Air Force's back office systems, whether it was supply or logistics or medical or finance, and that was my specialty. They had me working in finance arena. That was all organically grown with military uniform personnel riding the code. So it was we were on the ground floor of some of the really innovative capabilities in computer science in those days.",
