  {
    "episode_id": 91985,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91985.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 60,
    "turn_text": "certainly two things. Well, there were actually two operations. During the Gulf War, I was stationed at the Air Force's, and what was called the Standard System Center, where all of the Air Force's big systems were developed, whether it was finance, logistics, security, medical, all of those. And I was the executive officer to the commander there. And so we were involved in all of the light, the combat ammunition system, the system that ran the flight line and manages the maintenance on engines, airframes. We touch software that dealt with every aspect of the Air Force's operational mission. And so, from that, in that regard, I was very involved in the background, touching so many things that ultimately benefited the warfighter overseas. In the mid-90s, at Eglin Air Force Base, I was as the commander of the communications squadron there. We had what was called the Wing Initial Communications Package, and we had a contingent of about 14 airmen who would deploy as part of the advanced package any time that a fighter wing deployed. And when we went into Haiti back in the mid-90s, ultimately we never actually conducted the operation because the dictator there decided to leave and to surrender. So that was a good thing. But my unit provided the support. During the Cobar Towers bombing in 1996, I had two of my airmen in my unit that I had deployed with the 33rd Fighter Wing. That's where 19 Americans were killed. All of them were from my base. And one of my troops received a Purple Heart for his injuries. He actually lived in the dormitory that was blown up and barely made it out with his",
