  {
    "episode_id": 91985,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91985.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 63,
    "turn_text": "You know we didn't mainstream Air Force Up until 1997, we didn't talk about and deal with the terrorist threat like we do today. Certainly the Air Force today with its unmanned aerial vehicles and all of the capabilities that are there, very, very tactical, laser focused, precision strike capability. Even though we had some of that capability back then, You didn't hear the terms al-Qaeda or Hezbollah or Hamas or ISIL. You didn't think about it in those terms. Now when I left Eglun in 97 and went to U.S. Special Operations Command, that's where I began to learn that there was another evil element out there that we had to be. prepared for. And there was a lot of talk, but it wasn't just specifically Air Force. It was all of the services combined. You had the Navy SEALs, you had Army Special Forces, Air Force Special Operations, and of course Marines that were involved in all of that. I saw it grow and the emphasis on it grow, but really it began to take off, I think, around the 2000 time frame when I retired.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
