  {
    "episode_id": 91985,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91985.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 66,
    "turn_text": "It was a very rare opportunity. It was in the vernacular of the Air Force. It was called a joint assignment and it was a rare exception to be selected for that kind of position. You had to go through an interview process. And I felt very, very fortunate to have been selected. I ultimately wound up as the director of the CIO staff, the chief information officer staff working for an army colonel. And the head of our department, the actual CIO, was a Navy Admiral who had been the first commander of the Navy Seals Training School. and had been a Navy SEAL himself, or was a Navy SEAL himself. And my job was working all of the communicating and computing programs for special operations, whether it was Army, Air Force, Navy, air to ground. ground to ground and I had two senior officers from each service and two senior civilians that worked on my staff and we ran all of those programs and so we were involved for example in Y2K and making sure that all of the embedded systems were capable of handling the the turnover of the clock from 99 to 2000. So it was a phenomenal two years. Fast paced, I remember it very fondly and met some of our nation's real heroes working there. several times in my career I was told to get ready, but then when it came time to get on the airplane to go, they decided that we, the mission was going a different direction and I didn't have to deploy. So that's actually a regret that I have, that I was never able to deploy into an operational theater. You know, there's something about the military, particularly the Air Force. It really is a family. There's something that happens to an individual when they're broken down as an individual and built back up to be a stronger person and yet capable of operating as a team. There's a lot of comfort and there's a lot of motivation to do your best because you know other people are depending upon you. So it was an experience that I will never, ever forget.",
