  {
    "episode_id": 91985,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91985.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 69,
    "turn_text": "Well, you know, I'm not the president, but I would tell you what I believe, based on my experience, should be done. First of all, the president should be listening very, very closely to our senior military commanders to assess the threat and to get advice on what we need to destroy that threat. And I do believe that ISIL needs to be destroyed, not downgraded, not ultimately destroyed. The President should be building an international coalition of our friends and allies that believe strongly in the Western values that we have, because anyone who doesn't understand that this evil is threatening American values and our way of life, then we're burying our collective heads in the sand. And thirdly, the President should come to Congress and say, look, this is what our military leadership says we need to destroy this threat. And this is what we should provide, and I want to work with Congress to do that. That's what we need to be doing. This is not something that we can sit and wait on. This terrorist threat is engulfing the entire world, and we've got to face it for what it is. It's a war with radical Islam, and we have to call it what it is. Reagan brought down the Soviet Empire, and he brought down the wall. by calling the Soviet Empire what it was, the evil empire. You can't fight an enemy that you can't identify. And so I think it's very, very important that we show leadership, that we show, that we're strong enough to handle this because the other countries and the peoples of other countries that share our values are watching very closely to see whether or not America is going to continue to stand watch.",
